'''United States gubernatorial elections''' were held on November 7, 2023, in the states of [[Kentucky]] and [[Mississippi]], with an election having occurred in [[Louisiana]] on October 14. These elections form part of the [[2023 United States elections]]. The last regular gubernatorial elections for all three states were in [[2019 United States gubernatorial elections|2019]].

In Louisiana, term-limited incumbent [[John Bel Edwards]] was succeeded by [[Jeff Landry]], flipping the seat from [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] to [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] control. In Kentucky, Democrat [[Andy Beshear]] won re-election to a second term. In Mississippi, Republican [[Tate Reeves]] won re-election to a second term.

== Partisan composition ==
Going into the election, there were 26 Republican governors and 24 Democratic governors in the United States. This class of governors was made up of 1 Republican and 2 Democrats. Democrats were defending two governorships in states [[Donald Trump]] won in 2020 (Kentucky and Louisiana).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Vakil |first=Caroline |date=2023-01-02 |title=Here are the governors' races to watch in 2023 |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3791114-here-are-the-governors-races-to-watch-in-2023/ |access-date=2024-01-05 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Jacobson |first=Louis |date=June 8, 2023 |title=Democrats Face Uphill Challenge in Three Off-Year Races for Governor |url=https://www.usnews.com/news/elections/articles/2023-06-08/democrats-face-uphill-challenge-in-three-off-year-races-for-governor |access-date=January 5, 2023 |work=[[US News & World Report]]}}</ref>

==Election predictions==

Several sites and individuals published predictions of competitive seats. These predictions looked at factors such as the strength of the [[incumbent]] (if the incumbent is running for re-election), the strength of the candidates, and the partisan leanings of the state (reflected in part by the state's [[Cook Partisan Voting Index]] rating). The predictions assigned ratings to each seat, with the rating indicating a party's predicted advantage in winning that seat.

Most election predictors use:
* "<u>tossup</u>": no advantage
* "<u>tilt</u>" (used by some predictors): advantage that is not quite as strong as "lean"
* "<u>lean</u>": slight advantage
* "<u>likely</u>": significant, but surmountable, advantage 
* "<u>safe</u>" or "<u>solid</u>": near-certain chance of victory
