{{main|2023 Kentucky gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Kentucky|Governor]] [[Andy Beshear]] was elected in [[2019 Kentucky gubernatorial election|2019]] with 49.2% of the vote and ran for re-election to a second term.<ref name=KYCandidates>{{Cite web |title=Election Candidate Filings - Governor |url=https://web.sos.ky.gov/CandidateFilings/default.aspx?id=27 |access-date=May 3, 2023|website=web.sos.ky.gov}}</ref> He won the Democratic nomination with little opposition & won the general election.

[[Attorney General of Kentucky|Attorney general]] [[Daniel Cameron (American politician)|Daniel Cameron]], former [[List of ambassadors of the United States to the United Nations|US Ambassador to the UN]] [[Kelly Craft]], [[Kentucky State Auditor|state auditor]] [[Mike Harmon (politician)|Mike Harmon]], and agriculture commissioner [[Ryan Quarles]] all ran for the Republican gubernatorial nomination.<ref name="KYCandidates" /> Cameron was supported by former [[President of the United States|president]] [[Donald Trump]], and led every poll leading to the primary, with Craft and Quarles trailing behind him.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://spectrumnews1.com/ky/louisville/news/2022/06/17/daniel-cameron-donald-trump|title=Trump endorses Cameron's bid for governor in Kentucky|website=spectrumnews1.com|accessdate=April 6, 2023}}</ref> Cameron won the Republican nomination, becoming the first African American major party gubernatorial nominee in Kentucky's history.<ref>{{cite news |title=Daniel Cameron wins Republican primary for Kentucky governor |work=[[CBS News]] |date=May 16, 2023 |access-date=May 17, 2023 |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/daniel-cameron-wins-republican-primary-kentucky-governor/}}</ref>

