[[Governor of Louisiana|Governor]] [[John Bel Edwards]] was re-elected to a second term in [[2019 Louisiana gubernatorial election|2019]] with 51.3% of the vote. He was [[Term limits in the United States#Gubernatorial term limits|term-limited]] by the [[Constitution of Louisiana|Louisiana Constitution]] in 2023, and could not seek reelection for a third consecutive term.

Louisiana utilizes a [[jungle primary]] system. Republican [[List of Attorneys General of Louisiana|Attorney General]] [[Jeff Landry]] was considered the frontrunner. Other candidates included [[Louisiana State Senate|state senator]] [[Sharon Hewitt]], and [[Louisiana State Treasurer|Treasurer]] [[John Schroder]]. Democratic Secretary of Transportation [[Shawn Wilson (politician)|Shawn Wilson]] also ran to succeed Edwards.<ref>{{cite web |last=Karlin|first=Sam|title=Billy Nungesser to announce he's not running for governor, will seek re-election |url=https://www.theadvocate.com/baton_rouge/news/politics/elections/billy-nungesser-to-announce-hes-not-running-for-governor-will-seek-re-election/article_5cd6018a-9053-11ed-9089-678da36fdfa2.html|website=The Advocate|date=9 January 2023|access-date=11 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Doyle |first1=Matt |title=2023 Governor's race could be packed with GOP heavy hitters |url=https://louisianaradionetwork.com/2019/11/19/2023-governors-race-could-be-packed-with-gop-heavy-hitters/ |access-date=November 29, 2020 |work=[[Louisiana Radio Network]] |publisher=[[Louisiana Farm Bureau Federation]] |date=November 19, 2019}}</ref>

Landry won with over 51% of the vote in the primary, becoming the first person to win a Louisiana gubernatorial election without a runoff since [[Bobby Jindal]] in [[2011 Louisiana gubernatorial election|2011]]. Landry easily prevailed over several Republican opponents and the leading Democratic candidate.<ref>{{cite news |work=[[Politico]] |access-date=October 14, 2023 |date=October 14, 2023 |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/10/14/jeff-landry-wins-louisiana-governor-00121598 |last=Montellaro |first=Zach |title=Landry wins Louisiana governor's race, flipping state red}}</ref> All the Republican candidates collectively won 65.52% of the vote, including a candidate who got 0.34% of the vote as he dropped out of the race but remained on the ballot. All the Democrats collectively won 28.53% of the vote, and all the Independents collectively won 5.95% of the vote. This was the only governorship to flip parties in the 2023 elections.

